the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/c5/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3e495a-4d39-5ca6-b3be-a0813078a0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278868} {:answer/id #uuid "a23d32b3-d4d4-5e82-8301-ae2d3c9a6002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280033} {:answer/id #uuid "d5175981-dd9b-5a24-a6b2-37545e23ce11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9e/9ecab1b0f048e83b57d04a60c1cf20ee5ed0e61257577cf42c1a091976c04473.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280472} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9b7a18-0143-5fce-848a-c380d264cf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7f/7fc8ce1e1eaa3eb4e4acbbc167bdc4a479591bea21d2acdeaf799bd99d6b1150.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280544} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be8a96-29fb-524c-92e1-8f2da938314c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280033} :answer-field/id #uuid "262596a8-f5e0-5444-9e30-382a5a13609e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee68f9ff-69b4-5317-9406-24330023ed30" :question/math-academy-id "q-161855" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a27b376-f76e-5224-bd14-dcd0f58ef17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279419} {:answer/id #uuid "6df88852-e281-540b-9be8-5da79ff04db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279592} {:answer/id #uuid "e29c7966-53cc-5a98-a45d-f216ac316386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280594} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bf82bf-7ae4-51b0-8078-b62137d694c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280619} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae113f-b190-5e88-a500-9aa18217b4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279592} :answer-field/id #uuid "150f9199-b68c-5aef-8efe-e079ecbd49f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ee4cd0d-00ad-5fb0-be36-91eca3f6f7a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161873" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d9515a0-7aca-5bd0-93c2-b5138c7c3052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279294} {:answer/id #uuid "62fb6c42-d7b5-5116-b4d8-c608240b0020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279491} {:answer/id #uuid "65e00832-3370-516f-bf0e-931f9e463a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279521} {:answer/id #uuid "c042a147-a78a-546c-adff-8d9859d6b5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280284} {:answer/id #uuid "df364235-b831-5c0a-a5fa-907e835f3022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279491} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8c07a6-1fb4-5da8-abe5-ac2081b48db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9201622d-d447-58b4-8006-2fbea11393d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161874" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(4,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{u}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018385f7-7519-5922-8545-718d1e4443e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278597} {:answer/id #uuid "09f72da4-93f7-5f6e-8f5c-7adf97a2d87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278688} {:answer/id #uuid "42229dfb-92db-527f-98d4-81e464debdce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279182} {:answer/id #uuid "5806f5cb-b442-516d-a7af-f295ed7a67a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279401} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f49ff-6805-5e3d-9a84-bb5bf0cb46d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278688} :answer-field/id #uuid "30341de0-d7c9-531a-935d-0764a34a14a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a571cf4-d621-5149-95d3-896e62b990d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161875" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(0,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u-{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6868218d-e1cc-5d50-a993-c991f43b1d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279541} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc9479c-c9e8-5ef9-bf90-29e06a33263c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279705} {:answer/id #uuid "9162be54-7854-53e2-9565-c82bf2b82b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279888} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ff9956-5830-5df1-9e06-3627aaf262d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280480} {:answer/id #uuid "d96ff063-ffb7-5f8f-95e7-79745e683d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf437f98-16a0-57d1-a983-d2d8546f1bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a48d509-ce9f-57c3-ab71-29eb65410f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161885" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =\\left(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v}\\right)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cbff252-78de-56e0-9831-68d0d5847cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278858} {:answer/id #uuid "254166ee-c366-5b0a-8254-ef1c99d8cefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278923} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7b2391-4ac4-5aba-b7fd-d5c4a0c731ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279258} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb16338-8739-5100-afb4-a97df7a35f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279437} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec2c7bb-fc96-59d3-9cef-a3d5103aac04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279437} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c1b0c7-bd87-588b-918a-c6b0538039e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "787ef4d5-9dfe-5be7-87e1-4a46bed285bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161887" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03a648-3276-51bd-9904-df1d4522549b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278709} {:answer/id #uuid "0d190dfe-46ce-5dc3-b82b-670060383d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278713} {:answer/id #uuid "2f78edf6-ba63-5eb2-ac9e-cec1baa76b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279006} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9d76f8-5364-55b7-bd19-13182f53be56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279732} {:answer/id #uuid "dacdb6c9-01bb-5071-9c74-09822a99f555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278713} :answer-field/id #uuid "565bb2c5-d8e2-5a10-ab5d-91f31338f51b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c14a191e-1e95-5ff6-80a2-30d79cf0bf66" :question/math-academy-id "q-162220" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2699a966-6b46-57cf-a47c-22a3c667651c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278937} {:answer/id #uuid "32391712-510d-5554-9a81-b26d6930e9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279032} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcd3745-9952-55fa-8d98-6a218c3df910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279695} {:answer/id #uuid "d4655d09-3913-5973-b875-0e396a2fcd45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280464} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa048d-a79a-598e-9189-af11a04e4488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d03b1767-6387-503d-af9b-02fb2cde0ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd877a62-b477-55ef-9e5a-194da8c5c887" :question/math-academy-id "q-162230" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =((\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}-(-\\pi )^{4}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f453c1-1269-5d7b-a9f7-d8c20a89e598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279168} {:answer/id #uuid "7c949669-3c80-5476-91b1-fc3b5d631b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279704} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e227ef-2347-5b50-9e1d-289d72ee8083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280068} {:answer/id #uuid "d01a006d-cf1e-5f29-84c5-eff90c285d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280422} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aa8cae-8f41-5bb7-8dbc-0f01c9e8efb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8079c5b-3e54-561a-bcb4-10bee6629161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13da6f40-f5bf-549b-a97f-20a44b83344f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162235" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =(1-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2t)+0\\cdot 2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-\\frac{1}{4})-(-1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1107e5cc-b9e4-51b4-b110-09cc2b0cd618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278747} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffec3d9-ca96-573b-8802-6b5844531fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279155} {:answer/id #uuid "92a000d5-a346-5920-a484-8ed38558a27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279894} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0e41b-2046-5460-a0ac-9936085c2a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280693} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b31ed2-8b79-55de-ae35-ed2256356bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5895b727-7ab1-5288-96b4-772760ec6b05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7cf2656-c8b3-55d8-81c3-17aef82ea92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162237" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t. \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27840c28-45a9-5916-b4c1-d818db73736e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278944} {:answer/id #uuid "70f20afe-3ba7-5528-8071-2b9eaa046011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279616} {:answer/id #uuid "a723a97b-4d1b-542c-b720-ec65cd2786a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} {:answer/id #uuid "ddabc804-5099-5814-b0bc-e09a67f7767a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280545} {:answer/id #uuid "e94716b8-3388-57e0-901d-e8160a0aacf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-5}{103}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} :answer-field/id #uuid "559ef114-250d-5d31-9814-ea273f22c26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e15967c-9185-5388-a8b6-baaf9a1de47a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162316" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j+({t}^{3}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125e28c0-bbb7-5d93-95b9-69fe92e1f551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278759} {:answer/id #uuid "3eefc9e2-c929-54c3-9081-01b14d2b4379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279147} {:answer/id #uuid "5764f77b-9cd0-5e34-8a78-c0ca6ff9d828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279393} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7cfdf-beb6-5886-9078-290c6cf747ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10f5c-6829-52c8-b539-d1d5a5320bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f8a8c0-e049-5f88-9126-dfef6847e2bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "268c6ade-bfb9-5104-a3ea-6d4f751fe4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162324" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f463057-f805-5d6c-b391-8d33bfb0e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278729} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f0c4c-50ad-5a82-9743-532f6b798a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,-2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279595} {:answer/id #uuid "77ebb165-94ff-572d-849d-60ea7ccc9364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279662} {:answer/id #uuid "ba62a950-4c28-54b0-850e-c0240391ff63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} {:answer/id #uuid "dff98bc2-4344-5634-a1af-59494d77062c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,-2xy-1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280569}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} :answer-field/id #uuid "010aaa59-5dc6-5e8e-a83f-1b98c7c0cffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "240db9c9-76c2-5c97-8e69-9ca188d1d1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162361" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db0748b-118a-5639-b8f8-101df0972775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2fab1f-e285-51f4-a3b5-fff44aa9419e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279130} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0afc91-4186-596b-a560-07301b9b3b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279689} {:answer/id #uuid "820edc0c-36f3-5234-a0a6-bb7079f70fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi +{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279757} {:answer/id #uuid "8f71466e-d558-54ce-b28e-4bc0ccfab292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbd89c4-cb59-5b1c-a88e-c10bdb94a980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ae18124e-1564-593a-8b3e-a861867113b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162366" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x{e}^{4}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{4}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5032ce38-7144-56f3-8abf-062a36b12bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279325} {:answer/id #uuid "83b488ab-928c-5811-9c80-8d860a67a666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279771} {:answer/id #uuid "9b85bad0-7c0c-5cc8-b1a9-087dcba76ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279970} {:answer/id #uuid "e475d0cc-7991-51cb-aee6-efc92dbc2dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7ed414-d61d-5709-ad35-0b55e3209b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e171a0c-c922-554a-8200-5911711795ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db5de703-3efe-5274-901b-09592c12a8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-162374" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩,$ and $C$  is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d80d2d-e154-51fd-a594-5eba0a5e6b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278765} {:answer/id #uuid "14b8499c-89bd-5bc3-931b-c40e7ce37aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278777} {:answer/id #uuid "72b205e0-a52e-5333-be4c-5c18dbe481a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3aff6a-8638-5f77-8507-0d3f55b19bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280102} {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea93f9f9-61f9-5ba5-a529-0a7b5d54ca7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "915ccbd6-d5b8-5b79-ba41-8aee577baac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162886" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a49d17-1ccd-5d21-8067-3cc28c01dfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279659} {:answer/id #uuid "9498af25-8e31-5391-9c5d-57b5a039adb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279913} {:answer/id #uuid "9af2af40-8464-54ca-9c05-b548baff602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279964} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e4b95-0c02-5828-a503-1bc5e276aa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} :answer-field/id #uuid "98a966d4-0bb1-5c55-b273-7cce49dd0df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c852df75-6989-5a7d-bc7f-0618226da77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162887" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-1)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k)\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5b07f3-4813-5a4a-a0e6-a5bf77edd48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278852} {:answer/id #uuid "24161e0a-0b08-5db9-9481-589c38c2931e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278915} {:answer/id #uuid "3927e74a-4b53-5c18-92e5-d51492930769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279093} {:answer/id #uuid "bc856658-afbb-5fe8-bdf2-8d82839c6b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} {:answer/id #uuid "f923d8f6-fbaa-5b3e-a1a9-91366f82223e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cbe268-3f0b-5f24-95dd-226717656d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "203be68d-096d-564c-bf44-7d62e44d7418" :question/math-academy-id "q-162892" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0890c131-5eeb-5f67-aa90-086374575a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281763} {:answer/id #uuid "18e0c24c-52d2-5c41-a933-2d13b3e8afa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281913} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebf50dd-9fc4-55fb-91d9-28de2bacb253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281964} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba9ca97-13f2-5cf7-9f0e-df8b3d876788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282614} {:answer/id #uuid "97883037-baea-5b8b-9a40-b05574105c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281913} :answer-field/id #uuid "63fe44b0-587e-52d7-9525-c7108d7f5367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdeb0ff5-45e6-5343-a0cd-f339e82d372b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16451" :question/problem "![](images/61/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d9bb51-7d28-5203-a100-671bfb7b1984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281995} {:answer/id #uuid "a39c00a4-afcb-520f-b854-4bc1d26a2c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} {:answer/id #uuid "b2234da2-6880-5948-bede-cdef269e6b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283232} {:answer/id #uuid "cbccc52e-55e7-5e83-85bf-7ec1fde391a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283470} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3590a4-fe8c-559b-a199-73736d3c5178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a67a626-3555-5355-acc5-d65f47afb3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc47bd40-4608-5f54-a026-2eadf6dc5b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-16452" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ea4dfa-3746-512d-a4e5-fa19281f7cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281707} {:answer/id #uuid "119639f4-439c-5028-9a09-08e66ef5732b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281856} {:answer/id #uuid "376b6ab6-79ed-53f0-aaa3-87bd75aa6c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282182} {:answer/id #uuid "66196dd8-4f71-57e1-8517-24340f5f07aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} {:answer/id #uuid "95c0623c-a78b-5fec-8c1e-678b85527dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} :answer-field/id #uuid "29260e1c-0c08-5549-8699-d160c488888a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a341dec-953c-5c37-b7a1-a5cd95c6de7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16454" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21022c7d-b6d6-5cae-a0a4-a57d89afab0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285086} {:answer/id #uuid "49921218-a78e-55ed-9401-67d5dff3f875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285465} {:answer/id #uuid "5f92b6ee-a669-5379-82fa-e12d81b14834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285665} {:answer/id #uuid "62d465ea-c086-5e0e-baf0-37c50f53cd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285692} {:answer/id #uuid "b073025c-0c72-5876-88dd-01988080f163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285465} :answer-field/id #uuid "43436436-3068-54a8-97bc-a4c069c2a82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa24bddc-180c-5ff0-88bc-f05ee3a66fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-18252" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $p$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 2p-4\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2p-4+10)-0+(-10-6) \\\\  & =2p-4+10-10-6 \\\\  & =2p-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-10 & =0 \\\\ 2p & =10 \\\\ p & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=5.$"}]}
